1.To add elements to an ArrayList, use the add() method: array_list_variable.add(value)
2.You can also add an element at a specified position by referring to the index number: array_list_variable.add(index, value)
3.To access an element in the ArrayList, use the get() method and refer to the index number: array_list_variable.get(index)
4.To modify an element, use the set() method and refer to the index number: array_list_variable.set(index,new_value)
5.To remove an element, use the remove() method and refer to the index number: array_list_variable.remove(index)
6.To remove all the elements in the ArrayList, use the clear() method: array_list_variable.clear()
7.To find out how many elements an ArrayList have, use the size method: array_list_variable.size()
8.Loop through the elements of an ArrayList with a for loop, and use the size() method to specify how many times the loop should run
9.You can also loop through an ArrayList with the for-each loop
10.useful class in the java.util package is the Collections class, which include the sort() method for sorting lists alphabetically or numerically: Collections.sort(array_list_variable)
11.From Java 10, you can use the var keyword to declare an ArrayList variable without writing the type twice. The compiler figures out the type from the value you assign.
// Without var
ArrayList<String> cars = new ArrayList<String>();
// With var
var cars = new ArrayList<String>();
12.The List Interface: List<String> cars = new ArrayList<>();
This means the variable (cars) is declared as a List (the interface), but it stores an ArrayList object (the actual list). Since ArrayList implements the List interface, this is possible.
It works the same way, but some developers prefer this style because it gives them more flexibility to change the type later.